  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/hippichthys-spicifer-barbelly-pipefish",
      "html": "<ul><li>Genus: Hippichthys (river pipefish)</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern</li><li>Origin: wild caught</li><li>Max size: up to about 18 cm, but very slim</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/hippichthys-spicifer-barbelly-pipefish",
      "html": "<p>Hippichthys spicifer, the Bellybar or Banded Freshwater Pipefish, is a relative of seahorses found across the Indo-Pacific, from the Red Sea and East Africa to Samoa.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/hippichthys-spicifer-barbelly-pipefish",
      "html": "<p>It lives in mangroves, tidal creeks and the lower freshwater reaches of rivers, moving between fresh, brackish and marine water.</p><p>Body shape: long, thin, armored body made of bony rings, with a tube-shaped snout. Fins: small dorsal fin used for slow, upright movement; tiny tail fin. Coloration: brown to reddish above with fine mottling and pale spots, bluish below with dark bars across the belly. Some dark adults lose the bars.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/hippichthys-spicifer-barbelly-pipefish",
      "html": "<ul><li>TDS: 300+ ppm; a salt addition is useful. Keepers report they have done well both with a little salt and in fully fresh water.</li><li>pH: 7.0 to 8.4</li><li>Temperature: 22 to 28°C</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/hippichthys-spicifer-barbelly-pipefish",
      "html": "<p>Carnivore that needs live or frozen food. Baby brine shrimp are taken well. Also offer small mysis, copepods and other small invertebrates in small, frequent meals. Keep flow low or they will have a hard time catching food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/hippichthys-spicifer-barbelly-pipefish",
      "html": "<p>Generally peaceful and very slow-moving, staying in the lower zone of a densely planted tank. Very peaceful: keep in a species-only tank or with other calm, non-aggressive tankmates that will not compete for food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/hippichthys-spicifer-barbelly-pipefish",
      "html": "<p>Males carry the eggs in a brood pouch under the tail and release live young. It is not known whether the young need brackish or salt water to grow to adulthood.</p>"
    }
  ],
